WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:02.320
This is Atlanta, Georgia.

00:00:02.320 --> 00:00:03.360
It's my hometown.

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:05.440
It's also where I became a sports fan.

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:08.320
I played some sports as
a kid and I love sports.

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:11.120
In fact, my first real job in journalism

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:13.920
was covering the 1996 Olympic Games.

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:15.440
I've changed a lot since then.

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:17.840
You can see it in my hairline alone.

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:21.360
But the city has too,
especially in Downtown Atlanta

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:24.480
which remains the epicenter
of sports in Atlanta.

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:27.600
The Hawks play within steps of The Falcons

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:29.440
and the college football
hall of fame is right

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:31.120
across Centennial Park.

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:32.640
Georgia Tech plays football at one

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:35.920
of the oldest stadiums in
the NCAA, just up the street.

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:40.000
And all of this is located in
the heart of Fulton County.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:41.600
You probably heard that name a few times

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:42.880
in the recent elections.

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:43.600
Fulton County.

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:44.720
From Fulton County.

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:45.520
Fulton County.

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:49.440
A big part of that difference
is in these counties,

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:51.680
in this Metro Atlanta area.

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:54.480
A solidly red state for almost 30 years,

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:57.040
back to when Bill Clinton
was first elected,

00:00:57.040 --> 00:01:00.080
Georgia was suddenly in play in 2020

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:03.200
and record voter turnout
ultimately played a key role

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:06.240
in determining the balance of
power in the United States.

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:08.800
But if you were living
in Atlanta last summer,

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:12.400
record-setting voting
seemed unlikely at best.

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:16.000
Seems like this, playing out
across Fulton County, Tuesday

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:18.720
starting early in the morning,
extending through midnight.

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:21.920
The June ninth primary
election is a primary example

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:24.080
of systematic depression that the people

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:26.320
of this County still face.

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:28.880
But far from disaster,
Fulton County became a model

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:31.040
of turnout and voter efficiency.

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:33.840
Along the way creating one of
the largest voting precincts

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:36.000
in the history of the United States.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:37.360
At the heart of the effort,

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:40.240
Atlanta sports franchises and athletes.

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:43.120
And while some owners would
open their doors literally

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:46.800
and figuratively to activism,
others would oppose it

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:48.960
and end up losing their teams altogether.

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:52.320
When you own a sports franchise,
hopefully any business,

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:53.840
but certainly a sports franchise,

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:59.440
you have an obligation to try
to be relevant to the public

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:01.840
and to the community you operate in.

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:05.760
And that's actually good business
and the right thing to do.

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:09.520
They just literally feel
a culture shift happening.

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:11.920
I felt like I was just
excited to be a part

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:13.600
of a city that stands for something.

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:18.000
By now we all know the results
of those key elections,

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:22.000
but behind the scenes were
a series of crucial moves.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:25.040
I wanted to hear from the
people who made those decisions

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:27.200
and who once again, turned Atlanta

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:35.840
into a microcosm of race,
politics and sports.

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:42.480
Now look, I know Atlanta
doesn't boast the championships

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:47.040
of New York, Boston or LA,
it's nowhere close, sadly,

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:49.760
but Atlanta's long been a sports town

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:52.160
and the history of its athletes and teams,

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:54.400
well it's inextricably tied

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:56.960
to its central role in
the Civil Rights Movement.

00:02:56.960 --> 00:03:00.080
Well, in my days in Atlanta as a child,

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:03.120
there was a pretty strict
system of segregation.

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:05.840
The birthplace of Martin Luther King, Jr.

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:08.080
And the home to luminaries
such as Andrew Young

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:09.760
and the late Congressman John Lewis.

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:13.920
Atlanta which once built itself,
the city too busy to hate,

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:16.400
has also been home to
some of the most exciting,

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:19.760
legendary and controversial
sports figures, franchises

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:21.840
and events of the past 50 years.

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:25.200
In 2020 with sports largely sidelined

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:26.640
by the global pandemic,

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:29.920
those Downtown Atlanta
teams didn't fare so well

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:32.400
even if there was no one there to see it.

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:36.240
The Hawks missed the playoffs
again and The Falcons, well,

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:38.400
they found new ways to lose,

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:40.000
but on those same courts and fields,

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:43.760
both athletes and owners may
generation defining decisions.

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:46.560
Now I wish I could take
you back to Atlanta,

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:48.400
show you all of this myself,

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:50.960
but the global pandemic
won't allow for that either.

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:52.720
So I'm going to do the best that I can

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:55.040
from here in my daughter's
bedroom, in New York.

00:03:56.480 --> 00:03:58.800
Okay, so let's look at the players here.

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:00.240
Here's Downtown Atlanta.

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:02.880
This is State Farm Arena,
it's where The Hawks play.

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:05.440
They're owned by a group
led by investor Tony Ressler

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:06.640
and his family.

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:09.760
The CEO is a native Atlantan
and longtime Coca-Cola

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:12.720
and Turner broadcasting
executive named Steve Koonin.

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:13.600
And up until recently,

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:15.520
the head coach of The
Hawks was Lloyd Pierce.

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:16.800
He's part of a new generation

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:18.960
of young activists NBA leaders.

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:21.760
All three of these men played a huge role

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:24.480
in turning the stadium
into a voting center.

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:28.320
Nearby is the giant newly
constructed Mercedes-Benz stadium.

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:29.760
This is where the Atlanta Falcons

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:32.800
along with major league soccer's
Atlanta United, both owned

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:35.520
by Home Depot co-founder
Arthur Blank, play their games.

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:38.400
It too would become an early voting center

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:39.920
in the Senate runoff elections.

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:42.560
Of course where to vote was critical,

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:45.120
but so too is getting people motivated.

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:47.680
And that's where the
city's athletes came in,

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:49.040
spurred by local leaders,

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:52.080
as well as the activist
heritage of Atlanta.

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:54.160
Few played a more notable role

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:56.880
than WNBA star Renee Montgomery.

00:04:56.880 --> 00:04:59.360
She gave up a year of
playing for the Atlanta Dream

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:02.240
in order to partner with
activist groups like Fair Fight

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:03.200
and More Than a Vote.

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:05.120
We'll get into all of that,

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:09.120
but where this story really
starts is late May of 2020

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:10.560
with the killing of George Floyd.

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:13.371
If there ain't no justice.

00:05:13.371 --> 00:05:14.344
No peace.

00:05:14.344 --> 00:05:18.560
Black lives matter, black lives matter.

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:21.360
Breonna Taylor gets shot
and killed in her own home.

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:24.080
Ahmaud Arbery gets shot and killed

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:26.720
while he's simply going
on a jog down Georgia.

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:30.480
George Floyd is choked
out and lynched on camera

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:33.200
in front of all of us to
witness live and in person.

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:38.960
And you have, you know,
millions of black people,

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:41.920
young black people in particular,
all around this country

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:45.120
who are upset or angry or frustrated.

00:05:45.760 --> 00:05:46.960
This is Michael Tyler.

00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:48.720
He also grew up in Atlanta

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:50.800
and now he's the executive vice-president

00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:52.080
of More Than a Vote.

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:54.880
That's a group started
by LeBron James in 2020

00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:57.840
to mobilize voters by tapping
athletes and entertainers

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:06.240
to spread the word, city
by city and state by state.

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:09.040
I want a righteous anger.

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:12.880
The key is to be able
to channel that anger.

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:16.240
I think a lot of the athletes themselves

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:17.680
felt the exact same way.

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:22.480
I think what it did was
crystallize for all of us the need

00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:24.400
to form an organization like this,

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:26.480
realizing that the best way to fight back

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:28.720
in this moment and to
create a more just society

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:31.440
for our communities is
through the electoral process.

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:33.920
When I think back to the summer of 2020,

00:06:33.920 --> 00:06:36.160
I just think back to all
the passion in the city.

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:44.960
You know, I was out there giving waters

00:06:44.960 --> 00:06:47.280
and having cookouts for the protesters.

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:49.920
There was a lot of
energy and I'm like, man,

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:52.160
we gotta make sure that
all these people vote.

00:06:52.160 --> 00:06:55.520
Renee Montgomery's a two
time WWE NBA champion

00:06:55.520 --> 00:06:57.840
and as of last summer,
she was the point guard

00:06:57.840 --> 00:06:59.120
for the Atlanta Dream.

00:06:59.120 --> 00:07:01.120
But in June she announced
she would opt out

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:04.320
of the 2020 season, deciding
to dedicate her time

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:06.480
to social justice and a big part

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:09.440
of her message was
relatively simple, vote.

00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:10.320
Big news this week

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:12.000
when Atlanta Dream guard Renee Montgomery

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:13.280
announced she was going to skip

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:15.680
the upcoming 2022 WNBA season.

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:17.680
I started to realize when
people are asking me,

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:19.840
are you excited about
this upcoming season?

00:07:19.840 --> 00:07:22.240
And I was like, I hadn't
even been thinking about it.

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:25.120
That started to trigger my
mind and maybe I need to follow

00:07:25.120 --> 00:07:28.720
you know, where my mind
is and where my heart is.

00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:32.640
I mean, it's not, it is not
a light decision at all.

00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:35.280
I mean, and listen, I'm
not a professional athlete,

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:38.000
you are, but I can only imagine that like,

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:40.720
you've got your routine,
you know what you gotta do,

00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:43.680
you literally know the steps
you have to take to get there,

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:45.040
to get the brass ring.

00:07:45.040 --> 00:07:47.840
This path is not so
clear in terms of like,

00:07:47.840 --> 00:07:49.760
how do you get people out to vote?

00:07:49.760 --> 00:07:50.400
What do they do?

00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:53.200
How did you go about saying, all right,

00:07:53.200 --> 00:07:54.880
this is how I spend my time.

00:07:54.880 --> 00:07:56.720
These are the projects that I wanna do?

00:07:56.720 --> 00:07:59.040
For me, I wanna help,
but I wanna make sure

00:07:59.040 --> 00:08:00.800
that I'm like helping in the right way.

00:08:00.800 --> 00:08:03.120
So I don't wanna just be
out there doing anything,

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:04.320
I wanted to make sure that, you know,

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:07.760
the messaging was what it should
be and that it was correct,

00:08:07.760 --> 00:08:09.920
it was factual and I had to lean on people

00:08:09.920 --> 00:08:11.360
that know what they're doing.

00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:13.200
So that's a More Than a Vote.

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:15.280
I remember Renee reaches out,

00:08:15.280 --> 00:08:17.360
she reached out to me personally via email

00:08:17.360 --> 00:08:19.840
and said, hey Michael, I
don't know if you've heard,

00:08:19.840 --> 00:08:22.480
I had heard, but I chose to step away

00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:24.000
from basketball full time.

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:26.560
I have to focus exactly
on these sorts of efforts.

00:08:26.560 --> 00:08:29.440
So, you know, whatever it
is that you need me to do,

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:30.080
I'm all in.

00:08:30.880 --> 00:08:35.600
Since that day, she has become
one of our top advocates

00:08:35.600 --> 00:08:38.160
for all of our work,
particularly down in Georgia,

00:08:38.160 --> 00:08:40.320
in Atlanta, which was ground zero

00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:42.080
for many of our efforts
in the general election.

00:08:42.720 --> 00:08:45.840
But with the pandemic in
full force all around her,

00:08:45.840 --> 00:08:48.160
boots on the ground was largely virtual.

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:50.080
And Montgomery said about mobilizing

00:08:50.080 --> 00:08:52.240
and motivating from her home.

00:08:52.240 --> 00:08:54.400
I know differences in
opinions on how to handle it

00:08:54.400 --> 00:08:56.400
and what to do, whatever
you do, just be safe.

00:08:56.400 --> 00:08:58.240
Protect you, protect your neighbor.

00:08:58.240 --> 00:08:59.360
We don't wanna hurt anybody.

00:08:59.360 --> 00:09:00.240
Keep the energy going.

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:02.480
Make it peaceful, but keep
it going, let's not stop.

00:09:03.280 --> 00:09:06.000
A room in her house would
get fully converted,

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:08.480
equipped with cameras, lighting,

00:09:08.480 --> 00:09:11.360
even space for photo
shoots and music recording.

00:09:11.360 --> 00:09:12.960
I have major studio envy.

00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:16.800
Like if you could see
like where I am, I'm like,

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:18.880
I'm actually in my daughter's bedroom.

00:09:18.880 --> 00:09:22.880
And I have like Anna and Elsa
over here, like it's just.

00:09:22.880 --> 00:09:25.280
Oh, you trying to build a
snowman over there, okay.

00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:27.440
You clearly have it down.

00:09:27.440 --> 00:09:30.160
Listen, this is the, welcome
to the new normal, right?

00:09:30.160 --> 00:09:31.600
Like this is the office, right?

00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:35.040
My home has become the studio.

00:09:35.600 --> 00:09:37.120
That means like for video,

00:09:37.120 --> 00:09:39.760
this is where I film
TMZ Sports at every day,

00:09:39.760 --> 00:09:41.280
right here in our studio.

00:09:41.280 --> 00:09:43.280
So it's kind of crazy to think about.

00:09:43.280 --> 00:09:45.120
My podcast is called Remotely Renee

00:09:45.120 --> 00:09:48.240
because I had to figure out
how to do everything remotely.

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:51.280
Montgomery would also create initiatives

00:09:51.280 --> 00:09:53.840
around increasing involvement
in local politics.

00:09:53.840 --> 00:09:56.880
She raised money to
improve education at HBCUs

00:09:56.880 --> 00:09:59.120
and spoke to a lot of people about voting.

00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:00.320
What do you tell young people

00:10:00.320 --> 00:10:02.080
and how are you encouraging
them to get out there,

00:10:02.080 --> 00:10:02.960
register and vote?

00:10:02.960 --> 00:10:03.520
One of the things

00:10:03.520 --> 00:10:05.440
that are gonna tackle
is voter suppression.

00:10:05.440 --> 00:10:07.440
As you spoke about earlier,
I'm here in Georgia.

00:10:07.440 --> 00:10:10.160
So that's my connection,
and my strong tie with .

00:10:10.160 --> 00:10:11.360
I'm excited to get things going

00:10:11.360 --> 00:10:13.280
with More Than a Vote campaign.

00:10:13.280 --> 00:10:15.440
I've heard people tell
me I'm not into politics,

00:10:15.440 --> 00:10:16.400
but I wanna remind them.

00:10:16.400 --> 00:10:19.360
You may not be into politics,
but politics is into you.

00:10:19.360 --> 00:10:21.680
It decides whether you
make a minimum wage.

00:10:21.680 --> 00:10:23.920
It decides whether you
get a stimulus check.

00:10:23.920 --> 00:10:25.280
It decides whether you get charged

00:10:25.280 --> 00:10:27.120
with a misdemeanor or felony.

00:10:27.120 --> 00:10:28.960
For Renee and for these
athletes who are at large,

00:10:28.960 --> 00:10:31.040
it speaks to their understanding

00:10:31.040 --> 00:10:32.320
of the power of their own voice.

00:10:33.280 --> 00:10:37.680
And they know that no matter
how individually they choose

00:10:37.680 --> 00:10:39.840
to leverage it, they
know that there's power.

00:10:39.840 --> 00:10:40.960
You know, it's synonymous

00:10:40.960 --> 00:10:42.800
with the way that LeBron
thinks about things too.

00:10:43.440 --> 00:10:45.760
When she stepped away, she
knew what she cared about.

00:10:45.760 --> 00:10:48.160
She knew what she wanted to
do, but she wanted to make sure

00:10:48.160 --> 00:10:50.800
that she had a specific plan of action

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:53.840
for how she was actually
going to execute her activism.

00:10:53.840 --> 00:10:54.720
At least speaking

00:10:54.720 --> 00:10:57.200
out on issues off the field or court

00:10:57.200 --> 00:10:59.440
hasn't always been well-received.

00:10:59.440 --> 00:11:01.520
Look no further than the way fans,

00:11:01.520 --> 00:11:05.520
national politicians and the
media treated Colin Kaepernick.

00:11:05.520 --> 00:11:07.920
After he spoke out
against racial injustice,

00:11:07.920 --> 00:11:10.560
he couldn't get a job,
despite being considered one

00:11:10.560 --> 00:11:12.320
of the top players at his position.

00:11:13.520 --> 00:11:15.760
LeBron of course was
famously told to quote,

00:11:15.760 --> 00:11:18.400
shut up and dribble, back in 2018,

00:11:18.400 --> 00:11:21.840
but his refusal to back down,
along with a growing chorus

00:11:21.840 --> 00:11:26.880
of black athletes, especially
in the WNBA and the NBA ensure

00:11:26.880 --> 00:11:29.200
that activism would be an integral part

00:11:29.200 --> 00:11:30.400
of their professional lives.

00:11:31.920 --> 00:11:34.160
Athletes are able to communicate

00:11:34.160 --> 00:11:36.800
with other young black people in a way

00:11:36.800 --> 00:11:39.360
that no politician can ever do.

00:11:39.360 --> 00:11:41.200
They're able to lay out mistakes

00:11:41.200 --> 00:11:43.280
and lay out their personal motivations

00:11:43.280 --> 00:11:45.040
in a way that is going to resonate

00:11:45.040 --> 00:11:48.320
with other young black
voters that is simply unique.

00:11:48.320 --> 00:11:50.720
And so when you have an entire coalition

00:11:50.720 --> 00:11:51.920
of athletes doing that,

00:11:51.920 --> 00:11:55.200
it becomes, you know, force
multiplier within our community

00:11:55.200 --> 00:12:00.000
and, you know, speaks to
the importance of voting

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:01.520
in a way that simply nobody else can.

00:12:02.720 --> 00:12:05.440
But while Montgomery was
working to get out the vote,

00:12:05.440 --> 00:12:08.320
those newly inspired folks had
to have somewhere to show up

00:12:08.320 --> 00:12:11.520
and vote and safely,
given the global pandemic.

00:12:12.240 --> 00:12:14.000
Today, voters in Georgia were met

00:12:14.000 --> 00:12:16.560
with long lines and
confusion at polling places

00:12:16.560 --> 00:12:17.840
as they tried to cast ballots

00:12:17.840 --> 00:12:19.440
in the state's primary elections.

00:12:19.440 --> 00:12:22.160
From the air, you could see
long lines of Georgia voters,

00:12:22.160 --> 00:12:24.800
socially distanced, stretching
through parking lots.

00:12:24.800 --> 00:12:25.760
They hold the line,

00:12:25.760 --> 00:12:28.480
the machines don't work
and we're just stuck here.

00:12:28.480 --> 00:12:30.480
Thousands waited hours to cast ballots

00:12:30.480 --> 00:12:32.000
and frustration was evident.

00:12:32.000 --> 00:12:33.600
Please God, help us.

00:12:33.600 --> 00:12:35.600
Fulton County being the largest county

00:12:35.600 --> 00:12:36.720
in the state of Georgia,

00:12:37.360 --> 00:12:39.600
therefore we're the
focal point to anything

00:12:39.600 --> 00:12:41.440
that goes right or wrong.

00:12:41.440 --> 00:12:42.480
This is Rob Pitts.

00:12:42.480 --> 00:12:45.120
He's the chairman of Fulton
County Board of Commissioners.

00:12:45.760 --> 00:12:48.320
Elected go wrong in those
early June primaries

00:12:48.320 --> 00:12:51.520
and in a state with a long
history of voter suppression,

00:12:51.520 --> 00:12:55.040
long wait lines disproportionately
affected black citizens.

00:12:55.040 --> 00:12:57.200
For Chairman Pitts, the
pandemic was creating

00:12:57.200 --> 00:12:59.600
both a health and a political crisis.

00:13:00.400 --> 00:13:03.440
Many of the precincts that
we had historically used

00:13:03.440 --> 00:13:06.240
and traditionally used
because of the Coronavirus,

00:13:07.200 --> 00:13:10.000
they'd said, well you can
no longer use our facility

00:13:10.000 --> 00:13:11.040
because we are afraid.

00:13:11.680 --> 00:13:15.120
Many of the workers who were
going to work on election day,

00:13:15.120 --> 00:13:17.520
in fact, six out of
seven at the last minute,

00:13:17.520 --> 00:13:19.440
decided that they were not gonna work

00:13:19.440 --> 00:13:20.320
because they were afraid.

00:13:20.880 --> 00:13:22.720
A primary vote is one thing,

00:13:22.720 --> 00:13:26.080
but one deck was a critical
series of elections.

00:13:26.080 --> 00:13:30.480
A runoff, a generationally
important presidential election

00:13:30.480 --> 00:13:33.840
and not one but two US Senate races.

00:13:33.840 --> 00:13:37.760
And tensions only increased
as a city long accustomed

00:13:37.760 --> 00:13:40.640
to complicated racial politics took part

00:13:40.640 --> 00:13:44.560
in a national reckoning around
inequality and injustice

00:13:44.560 --> 00:13:47.200
Tonight, anger and frustration in Atlanta.

00:13:47.200 --> 00:13:49.920
Crowds gathering outside
the fire gutted Wendy's

00:13:49.920 --> 00:13:52.560
where 27 year old Rayshard Brooks was shot

00:13:52.560 --> 00:13:55.280
and killed by police
during an attempted arrest.

00:13:55.280 --> 00:13:58.640
Within 24 hours, Atlanta's
police chief stepped down.

00:13:58.640 --> 00:14:00.880
The officer who shot Brooks was fired,

00:14:00.880 --> 00:14:02.960
and the other officer involved placed

00:14:02.960 --> 00:14:04.480
on administrative duty.

00:14:04.480 --> 00:14:06.960
The protest, they really
happened at the nexus

00:14:06.960 --> 00:14:10.400
of where our arena is, Marietta street

00:14:10.400 --> 00:14:13.040
and Centennial Olympic
Park, right on the park.

00:14:13.040 --> 00:14:15.040
Next to CNN is our building.

00:14:15.040 --> 00:14:16.080
This is Steve Koonin.

00:14:16.080 --> 00:14:19.200
He's the CEO of the Atlanta
Hawks and State Farm Arena.

00:14:19.200 --> 00:14:20.960
And in watching these protests,

00:14:20.960 --> 00:14:22.960
I was just struck with the passion.

00:14:22.960 --> 00:14:25.600
I was struck with the peaceful way

00:14:25.600 --> 00:14:28.400
that these young people
were out protesting.

00:14:28.960 --> 00:14:30.800
And to me, the only action,

00:14:30.800 --> 00:14:35.120
the only change that ever
comes is with voting.

00:14:35.120 --> 00:14:37.520
The most powerful thing
you can do is vote.

00:14:38.080 --> 00:14:40.880
And at the time, we were
sitting on an empty arena.

00:14:40.880 --> 00:14:44.160
No basketball, no concerts, no shows,

00:14:44.160 --> 00:14:48.160
but we had something in
our arena that is rare.

00:14:48.160 --> 00:14:50.000
You can find it very few places

00:14:50.000 --> 00:14:52.320
in that state, 700,000 square feet.

00:14:52.880 --> 00:14:55.520
We actually were sitting around again,

00:14:55.520 --> 00:14:56.720
there were others involved,

00:14:56.720 --> 00:14:59.360
and I guess as the owner,
you get to take credit

00:14:59.360 --> 00:15:02.160
for things that you really
didn't do or think of.

00:15:02.160 --> 00:15:05.520
So I'd be on that category, but
we were trying to figure out

00:15:05.520 --> 00:15:06.880
how can we help the community?

00:15:07.600 --> 00:15:09.840
This is the owner of
The Hawks, Tony Ressler.

00:15:09.840 --> 00:15:11.920
He made his fortune through Ares Capital,

00:15:11.920 --> 00:15:14.720
an investing firm he created in the 1990s.

00:15:14.720 --> 00:15:18.080
He put together a group to
buy The Hawks back in 2015,

00:15:18.080 --> 00:15:20.640
and while this main
residence is in Los Angeles,

00:15:20.640 --> 00:15:21.360
he and his wife,

00:15:21.360 --> 00:15:24.480
the actress Jamie Gertz have
become hands-on team owners.

00:15:25.120 --> 00:15:29.040
Why not use the arena
as a voting location,

00:15:29.040 --> 00:15:30.320
as a voting location.

00:15:30.320 --> 00:15:31.680
We weren't sure we could do it.

00:15:31.680 --> 00:15:34.080
And frankly, we weren't sure
Fulton County would permit us

00:15:34.080 --> 00:15:34.800
to do it.

00:15:34.800 --> 00:15:37.760
I absolutely remember getting
a call and phone rang.

00:15:37.760 --> 00:15:40.720
I have a flip phone, I have to .

00:15:40.720 --> 00:15:43.280
It rang and on the other hand

00:15:43.280 --> 00:15:46.080
was my good friend, Steve Koonin.

00:15:46.080 --> 00:15:48.640
So he said, well look, we're dark,

00:15:48.640 --> 00:15:50.400
there are no games playing here.

00:15:50.400 --> 00:15:52.800
We'd like to be being a
good corporate citizen.

00:15:53.520 --> 00:15:57.520
We would like to find out and
to know if there is a role

00:15:57.520 --> 00:16:01.840
that we at the Atlanta Hawks
and State Farm Arena can play.

00:16:02.400 --> 00:16:04.880
And I said Steve, let me get back to you.

00:16:05.440 --> 00:16:07.360
So in less than five minutes,

00:16:07.360 --> 00:16:11.040
I got back to him and I
said, okay we got a deal.

00:16:11.040 --> 00:16:12.960
Today officials from Fulton County

00:16:12.960 --> 00:16:14.880
and the Atlanta Hawks
announced the opening

00:16:14.880 --> 00:16:17.360
of Georgia's largest early voting precinct

00:16:17.360 --> 00:16:18.720
at State Farm Arena.

00:16:18.720 --> 00:16:21.360
The plan is to use it starting July 20th

00:16:21.360 --> 00:16:22.640
in the primary runoff

00:16:22.640 --> 00:16:25.440
as well as for the fall general election.

00:16:25.440 --> 00:16:27.120
I think we caught everybody off guard.

00:16:28.080 --> 00:16:31.040
And I think as soon as you hear the idea,

00:16:31.040 --> 00:16:33.200
you kind of go, well that makes sense.

00:16:33.200 --> 00:16:34.400
I mean, it's so simple.

00:16:34.400 --> 00:16:37.680
This is not rocket science
of an idea, it's simple.

00:16:38.320 --> 00:16:41.680
So the plan was the then
empty State Farm Arena

00:16:41.680 --> 00:16:43.120
would be turned into what was known

00:16:43.120 --> 00:16:45.200
as a super polling location.

00:16:45.200 --> 00:16:47.760
Meaning anyone in Fulton
County would be able to come

00:16:47.760 --> 00:16:49.440
to the arena to vote.

00:16:49.440 --> 00:16:51.360
The court at the arena would be dedicated

00:16:51.360 --> 00:16:53.760
to socially distanced voting machines,

00:16:53.760 --> 00:16:56.320
100 machines for the July runoff election

00:16:56.320 --> 00:16:58.080
and then tripling to 300

00:16:58.080 --> 00:17:00.560
for the general election in November.

00:17:00.560 --> 00:17:02.080
Conference rooms would be converted

00:17:02.080 --> 00:17:05.360
for poll workers to process
votes, including mail-in voting.

00:17:05.920 --> 00:17:08.720
The Hawks Foundation
would also provide 1,500

00:17:08.720 --> 00:17:09.920
free parking spots

00:17:09.920 --> 00:17:12.880
in the surrounding area
to accommodate voters.

00:17:12.880 --> 00:17:14.560
And in addition to the site,

00:17:14.560 --> 00:17:16.640
The Hawks added a critical piece,

00:17:16.640 --> 00:17:21.760
some 350 full-time staffers,
200 of whom would be deputized

00:17:21.760 --> 00:17:22.640
as poll workers.

00:17:24.160 --> 00:17:26.560
When you see election
problems, most of the time,

00:17:26.560 --> 00:17:29.120
it's because it's a
volunteer staff of people

00:17:29.120 --> 00:17:32.800
who don't have the
capability or the experience.

00:17:32.800 --> 00:17:34.960
We're in the customer service business.

00:17:34.960 --> 00:17:36.720
We know how to resolve issues.

00:17:36.720 --> 00:17:38.480
We're in the hospitality business,

00:17:39.040 --> 00:17:41.040
we're in the security business.

00:17:41.040 --> 00:17:44.240
So everybody felt highly
comfortable, quickly

00:17:44.800 --> 00:17:47.680
that we had the capability to do this.

00:17:49.040 --> 00:17:53.360
You need employee buy-in
to make this work.

00:17:53.360 --> 00:17:55.920
And the one thing that none of us want

00:17:55.920 --> 00:17:58.720
to acknowledge is the fear factor.

00:17:58.720 --> 00:17:59.840
You know, you commit your building

00:18:00.480 --> 00:18:04.800
and you become a worse polling location.

00:18:04.800 --> 00:18:06.480
You become a mess,

00:18:06.480 --> 00:18:09.440
you become a four-hour
wait and a disaster.

00:18:09.440 --> 00:18:11.840
Let's not kid ourselves,
that hurts your franchise,

00:18:11.840 --> 00:18:13.040
that hurts your business.

00:18:13.040 --> 00:18:14.560
That hurts your employee morale.

00:18:16.880 --> 00:18:17.680
But while the owners

00:18:17.680 --> 00:18:20.720
of The Hawks were busy creating
a super polling location,

00:18:20.720 --> 00:18:22.720
another basketball owner in Atlanta

00:18:22.720 --> 00:18:24.160
was making their own headlines.

00:18:24.160 --> 00:18:25.680
And it WNBA news,

00:18:25.680 --> 00:18:30.000
Atlanta Dream co-owner Kelly
Loeffler is not in favor

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:32.560
of the WNBA social justice plans.

00:18:32.560 --> 00:18:34.480
And Loeffler has sent a letter

00:18:34.480 --> 00:18:37.280
to commissioner Cathy Engelbert objecting

00:18:37.280 --> 00:18:38.640
to the league's initiatives

00:18:38.640 --> 00:18:40.640
to honor the Black Lives Matter movement.

00:18:41.200 --> 00:18:44.160
And Kelly Loeffler, of course,
wasn't just the co-owner

00:18:44.160 --> 00:18:45.520
of the Atlanta Dream,

00:18:45.520 --> 00:18:47.360
she was running to keep her seat

00:18:47.360 --> 00:18:49.680
as a US Senator from Georgia.

00:18:49.680 --> 00:18:51.520
Loeffler was objecting
to a league decision

00:18:51.520 --> 00:18:53.840
to allow players to wear
black warm-up jerseys

00:18:53.840 --> 00:18:57.360
with the words black lives
matter and say her name on them.

00:18:57.360 --> 00:18:59.280
In a letter to the league's commissioner,

00:18:59.280 --> 00:19:00.960
Loeffler argued that instead,

00:19:00.960 --> 00:19:03.600
they should place an American
flag on every Jersey.

00:19:03.600 --> 00:19:05.840
And she stated that
there needs to be less,

00:19:05.840 --> 00:19:08.480
not more politics in sports.

00:19:08.480 --> 00:19:10.560
For the Atlanta Dream for myself,

00:19:10.560 --> 00:19:13.360
you know, we saw where
we wanted to take a stand

00:19:13.360 --> 00:19:15.040
and just, and we did it.

00:19:15.040 --> 00:19:16.400
Last night, Elizabeth Williams,

00:19:16.400 --> 00:19:18.800
along with several teammates and players

00:19:18.800 --> 00:19:23.120
from other WNBA teams
wore a vote Warnock shirt.

00:19:23.120 --> 00:19:26.320
It was a very carefully
crafted message in support

00:19:26.320 --> 00:19:29.200
of US Senate candidate,
Reverend Raphael Warnock.

00:19:29.840 --> 00:19:32.640
Players from every team
in the WNBA would go on

00:19:32.640 --> 00:19:34.720
to wear shirts saying vote Warnock.

00:19:35.280 --> 00:19:37.040
So here, we've got to pause to marvel

00:19:37.040 --> 00:19:39.520
at a hard to make up
element of this story.

00:19:40.080 --> 00:19:43.040
The owner of a team
named to a vote the work

00:19:43.040 --> 00:19:45.760
of the late Dr. Martin Luther King Jr.

00:19:45.760 --> 00:19:49.040
effectively turned the
team against herself

00:19:49.040 --> 00:19:52.240
to the point where that
team endorsed her opponent.

00:19:52.800 --> 00:19:55.680
That opponent had the same job,

00:19:55.680 --> 00:19:58.400
pastor of the famed
Ebeneezer Baptist Church

00:19:58.400 --> 00:20:01.360
that the late Dr. King
and his father once held.

00:20:01.920 --> 00:20:03.760
But the Reverend Raphael Warnock was part

00:20:03.760 --> 00:20:06.880
of what they call a
non-partisan blanket primary.

00:20:06.880 --> 00:20:10.160
Part of a crowded field
looking to challenge Loeffler.

00:20:10.160 --> 00:20:12.160
And according to research
by the Washington Post,

00:20:12.160 --> 00:20:14.400
the exposure helped
boost Warnock's campaign

00:20:14.400 --> 00:20:15.440
at a critical time.

00:20:16.000 --> 00:20:20.000
48 hours after the WNBA players
started to wear his name,

00:20:20.000 --> 00:20:22.720
the campaign raised $183,000

00:20:22.720 --> 00:20:26.240
and attracted 3,500 new grassroots donors.

00:20:26.800 --> 00:20:29.920
And by the time the election
in November rolled around,

00:20:29.920 --> 00:20:33.520
Warnock was the only real
challenger left for Loeffler.

00:20:33.520 --> 00:20:37.440
We did our homework and
we used our platforms

00:20:37.440 --> 00:20:39.920
and those are the same
platforms that a lot of people

00:20:39.920 --> 00:20:43.920
you know, they tried to overlook,
but we all band together

00:20:43.920 --> 00:20:45.120
and we use our platforms

00:20:45.120 --> 00:20:46.320
for something that we believe in.

00:20:47.200 --> 00:20:48.400
And it was only a few weeks

00:20:48.400 --> 00:20:51.040
after the WNBA players endorsed Warnock

00:20:51.040 --> 00:20:54.560
that NBA athletes would grab
the sports world's attention.

00:20:54.560 --> 00:20:57.200
The Milwaukee Bucks
have reportedly decided

00:20:57.200 --> 00:21:01.120
to boycott game five
against the Orlando Magic

00:21:01.120 --> 00:21:04.560
in protest of the police
shooting of Jacob Blake.

00:21:05.680 --> 00:21:07.440
The Bucks would boycott the game

00:21:07.440 --> 00:21:09.920
after police shot and
paralyzed Jacob Blake

00:21:09.920 --> 00:21:11.520
in Kenosha, Wisconsin.

00:21:11.520 --> 00:21:14.960
The other teams in the bubble
in Orlando followed suit

00:21:14.960 --> 00:21:17.440
and NBA playoff games came to a halt

00:21:17.440 --> 00:21:20.080
along with most of the
rest of the sports world,

00:21:20.080 --> 00:21:22.640
from major league baseball to pro tennis.

00:21:22.640 --> 00:21:25.280
All eyes turned to the
NBA for the next move.

00:21:26.320 --> 00:21:28.800
The Milwaukee Bucks were the team

00:21:28.800 --> 00:21:30.800
that decided they weren't going to play,

00:21:30.800 --> 00:21:33.200
but it also took the rest of our leagues,

00:21:33.200 --> 00:21:34.800
the rest of our league
and the rest of the teams

00:21:34.800 --> 00:21:37.680
that were playing that day
to make that same decision.

00:21:37.680 --> 00:21:38.880
This is Lloyd Pierce.

00:21:38.880 --> 00:21:40.080
During the 2020 season,

00:21:40.080 --> 00:21:42.560
he was the head coach
for the Atlanta Hawks.

00:21:42.560 --> 00:21:43.760
This was in essence,

00:21:43.760 --> 00:21:45.760
the moment that we were preparing for.

00:21:45.760 --> 00:21:49.520
We had had our meetings as
coaches and trying to strategize

00:21:49.520 --> 00:21:53.600
on what we could do best
to support our players

00:21:53.600 --> 00:21:55.040
when the time was right.

00:21:55.040 --> 00:21:57.200
Well, that was the time,
that was the moment

00:21:57.200 --> 00:22:00.000
The Hawks, of course,
weren't in the NBA bubble

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:01.920
at the time, having missed the playoffs

00:22:01.920 --> 00:22:03.360
for the third year in a row.

00:22:03.920 --> 00:22:05.360
Have I mentioned that it's a little tough

00:22:05.360 --> 00:22:08.720
to be an Atlanta sports fan,
but The Hawks from afar,

00:22:08.720 --> 00:22:11.520
ended up playing a critical
role in the negotiations

00:22:11.520 --> 00:22:14.080
between the owners, the
league and the players.

00:22:14.640 --> 00:22:16.720
Two key agreements emerged.

00:22:16.720 --> 00:22:18.560
First, the league would
set up a new committee

00:22:18.560 --> 00:22:21.600
on racial justice and Coach
Pierce would head it up.

00:22:21.600 --> 00:22:27.520
I think the direct measure of
what we wanna do is leverage

00:22:27.520 --> 00:22:29.680
what we can do as a league
with all of our owners,

00:22:29.680 --> 00:22:31.760
all of our players, all of our coaches

00:22:31.760 --> 00:22:35.040
so that we can take the
action and the conversation

00:22:35.040 --> 00:22:38.000
from Orlando and amplify it into its real,

00:22:38.000 --> 00:22:41.280
tangible, direct action in our
community and in our country.

00:22:42.640 --> 00:22:45.120
The second thing spoke
directly to the voting issue

00:22:45.120 --> 00:22:47.600
which had become the most
tangible form of activism.

00:22:48.240 --> 00:22:52.080
Any NBA facility that could,
would become a voting center.

00:22:52.080 --> 00:22:53.840
And The Hawks had the blueprint.

00:22:56.480 --> 00:22:59.520
We had a lot of NBA teams,
whether it's from the strike.

00:22:59.520 --> 00:23:01.440
There were a lot of teams
that had been working on it

00:23:01.440 --> 00:23:02.640
prior to the strike.

00:23:02.640 --> 00:23:05.680
It was really encouraging to
see a lot of the organizations

00:23:05.680 --> 00:23:08.480
and the teams that represent
this country stand up

00:23:08.480 --> 00:23:10.880
and provide that same
opportunity across the country.

00:23:10.880 --> 00:23:14.640
There were several teams who
had wanted to do something

00:23:15.360 --> 00:23:19.680
in their community, and really
hadn't decided what to do.

00:23:19.680 --> 00:23:23.200
And it was just great communication
between team presidents

00:23:23.920 --> 00:23:26.480
that we sent out our
schematic and our budget.

00:23:26.480 --> 00:23:29.760
And, you know, the biggest
line item was security

00:23:29.760 --> 00:23:33.520
'cause you had to have a
lot of security this year.

00:23:33.520 --> 00:23:36.000
I mean that was the most,
the highest expense.

00:23:36.000 --> 00:23:37.280
More than 40 sports arenas

00:23:37.280 --> 00:23:38.800
in the US were ultimately converted

00:23:38.800 --> 00:23:41.600
to polling places during
the 2020 elections

00:23:41.600 --> 00:23:45.200
and some 300,000 people
around the country were able

00:23:45.200 --> 00:23:48.080
to vote in the general election
at NBA facilities alone.

00:23:48.720 --> 00:23:51.360
About 40,000 of those votes were all cast

00:23:51.360 --> 00:23:53.040
in State Farm Arena.

00:23:53.040 --> 00:23:55.920
Now keep in mind, the
presidential election in the state

00:23:55.920 --> 00:23:59.520
of Georgia was decided by
less than 15,000 votes.

00:24:02.080 --> 00:24:03.600
I think we really did create

00:24:03.600 --> 00:24:06.880
sort of the largest, safest,
most efficient polling location

00:24:06.880 --> 00:24:08.240
this country had ever seen.

00:24:08.240 --> 00:24:10.160
I think the responsibility

00:24:10.160 --> 00:24:14.240
from my perspective is really
to be a good community asset,

00:24:14.240 --> 00:24:17.680
to be hopefully a great
business, a great franchise,

00:24:17.680 --> 00:24:19.280
win basketball games

00:24:19.280 --> 00:24:21.280
and be a really positive
member of the community.

00:24:21.840 --> 00:24:25.600
And when both US Senate
races would require a runoff

00:24:25.600 --> 00:24:28.640
which would determine
control of the US Senate,

00:24:28.640 --> 00:24:31.120
this time The Hawks would
be joined by The Falcons

00:24:31.120 --> 00:24:33.440
and their home, Mercedes-Benz Stadium.

00:24:33.440 --> 00:24:35.680
When walked the stadium floor

00:24:35.680 --> 00:24:38.960
and I've walked our
stadium thousands of times

00:24:38.960 --> 00:24:40.480
as part of my job description.

00:24:40.480 --> 00:24:41.600
This is Steve Cannon,

00:24:41.600 --> 00:24:44.720
he's the CEO of AMB
Sports and Entertainment.

00:24:44.720 --> 00:24:47.520
The parent company that
Falcons in Atlanta United.

00:24:47.520 --> 00:24:50.400
He oversaw the election
process at Mercedes-Benz

00:24:50.400 --> 00:24:52.880
which would split duty
with State Farm Arena

00:24:52.880 --> 00:24:54.800
as a super polling location.

00:24:54.800 --> 00:24:57.600
But to come in on that
early voting exercise

00:24:57.600 --> 00:24:59.760
right where our stadium was transformed

00:24:59.760 --> 00:25:03.040
for a special purpose
in an important moment

00:25:03.040 --> 00:25:05.280
and to see how beautifully it ran.

00:25:05.280 --> 00:25:06.800
It was laid out well.

00:25:06.800 --> 00:25:11.280
There was a buzz and
conversations around our business

00:25:11.280 --> 00:25:12.720
among our associates.

00:25:12.720 --> 00:25:14.160
Hey, have you early voted yet?

00:25:14.160 --> 00:25:16.480
And how was your wait time?

00:25:16.480 --> 00:25:18.640
It was amazing that we were buzzing

00:25:18.640 --> 00:25:21.600
about this election moment because I feel

00:25:21.600 --> 00:25:27.040
like we've all recognized
the stakes of voting.

00:25:27.040 --> 00:25:30.960
And tell me a little bit
about the fans in this regard

00:25:30.960 --> 00:25:33.520
who I dare say, you know,

00:25:33.520 --> 00:25:37.680
reflect the changing
demographics of Atlanta.

00:25:37.680 --> 00:25:40.000
And I mean, there is an
element I would imagine

00:25:40.000 --> 00:25:42.320
to this that is enlightened self-interest.

00:25:42.320 --> 00:25:45.600
You wanna keep your fan base happy

00:25:45.600 --> 00:25:48.400
and reflect their values
and their wants and needs.

00:25:48.400 --> 00:25:49.280
Tell me about that.

00:25:49.280 --> 00:25:52.160
Well, at the end, people do business

00:25:52.160 --> 00:25:53.760
with entities that they like,

00:25:53.760 --> 00:25:56.320
they care about and that they feel reflect

00:25:56.320 --> 00:25:58.880
and share their values and businesses

00:25:58.880 --> 00:26:03.280
that do that will enjoy increasing
success with the mindset

00:26:03.280 --> 00:26:05.440
of this younger consumer that frankly

00:26:05.440 --> 00:26:07.040
right now is coming of age

00:26:07.040 --> 00:26:10.080
and are the most powerful
consuming cohort out there.

00:26:11.120 --> 00:26:14.000
The shift on the part of the
owners to not just embracing

00:26:14.000 --> 00:26:17.840
but encouraging activism,
can't be overstated.

00:26:17.840 --> 00:26:20.720
It has spread to the college
and even the high school level.

00:26:21.760 --> 00:26:24.160
It's also a reminder to those owners

00:26:24.160 --> 00:26:26.560
that this isn't just any old asset.

00:26:27.920 --> 00:26:30.640
So Tony, as an owner, it
feels like you witnessed

00:26:30.640 --> 00:26:32.480
a really important moment,

00:26:32.480 --> 00:26:35.600
a shift of sorts when it
comes to player empowerment,

00:26:35.600 --> 00:26:37.840
in terms of activism, in
terms of having a voice,

00:26:38.480 --> 00:26:41.840
it's not just tolerated
anymore, it's really expected.

00:26:42.480 --> 00:26:44.240
Listen, people live in the community

00:26:44.800 --> 00:26:46.480
that they play in, that they work in,

00:26:46.480 --> 00:26:47.520
that they contribute to.

00:26:48.160 --> 00:26:51.840
80% of NBA players are African-American.

00:26:53.040 --> 00:26:54.800
These are our business partners.

00:26:54.800 --> 00:26:57.360
So to me, we should try

00:26:57.360 --> 00:26:59.920
to help support our business partners

00:26:59.920 --> 00:27:02.480
in any way possible and our community.

00:27:02.480 --> 00:27:04.080
The idea of having a voice

00:27:04.080 --> 00:27:06.080
and the idea of being
good business partners

00:27:06.080 --> 00:27:08.400
and the idea of standing up
for what you think is right.

00:27:09.200 --> 00:27:10.640
I think that's all part

00:27:10.640 --> 00:27:12.640
of what the NBA is trying to achieve.

00:27:13.840 --> 00:27:16.160
In the runoff, Kelly Loeffler of course,

00:27:16.160 --> 00:27:17.760
lost that Senate seat.

00:27:17.760 --> 00:27:20.880
She ultimately decided
as well to sell her stake

00:27:20.880 --> 00:27:22.240
in the Atlanta Dream.

00:27:22.240 --> 00:27:25.440
And Renee Montgomery would
go from player to part owner.

00:27:26.080 --> 00:27:28.720
You know, it felt good to be again,

00:27:28.720 --> 00:27:31.120
a part of the right side of history

00:27:31.120 --> 00:27:32.160
where black women, you know,

00:27:32.160 --> 00:27:34.240
there's a lot of black women in the WMBA.

00:27:34.240 --> 00:27:37.760
You know, majority of
the league is minority.

00:27:37.760 --> 00:27:39.680
We understand the struggle.

00:27:40.240 --> 00:27:42.960
I think the days of kind
of shut up and play,

00:27:42.960 --> 00:27:44.240
you're here for our entertainment.

00:27:44.240 --> 00:27:45.680
Those days are over

00:27:45.680 --> 00:27:48.000
and that genie's never going back

00:27:48.000 --> 00:27:50.400
in the bottle and that's okay.

00:27:50.960 --> 00:27:52.640
And let's accept and celebrate it.

00:27:52.640 --> 00:27:55.120
The opportunity here
and the exciting part is

00:27:55.120 --> 00:27:57.680
that black voters in Georgia got a taste

00:27:57.680 --> 00:27:59.600
of what that feels like when you are able

00:27:59.600 --> 00:28:02.320
to flex your muscle and decide

00:28:02.320 --> 00:28:03.360
who your leaders are gonna be.

00:28:03.920 --> 00:28:06.080
2020, as we like to say
at More Than a Vote was

00:28:06.080 --> 00:28:07.360
just the tip off of our work.

00:28:08.000 --> 00:28:09.600
In fact, just a few months later,

00:28:09.600 --> 00:28:11.840
as LeBron and the rest
of the NBA were headed

00:28:11.840 --> 00:28:13.840
to Atlanta for the All-Star Game,

00:28:13.840 --> 00:28:17.520
the GOP controlled legislature
in Georgia was aiming

00:28:17.520 --> 00:28:19.200
to roll back some of the efforts

00:28:19.200 --> 00:28:21.920
that made it easier to vote in the state.

00:28:21.920 --> 00:28:23.440
One of the key activists,

00:28:23.440 --> 00:28:26.640
former Senator and former
Dream owner Kelly Loeffler.

00:28:27.200 --> 00:28:28.880
We don't know what's
gonna happen from here,

00:28:28.880 --> 00:28:32.240
but we understand things
that need to be fixed.

00:28:32.240 --> 00:28:33.520
You know, we're part of a system

00:28:33.520 --> 00:28:35.120
that probably needs to be fixed.

00:28:35.120 --> 00:28:36.000
So we get it.

00:28:36.000 --> 00:28:38.480
We get the idea of, you know,

00:28:38.480 --> 00:28:40.960
we have these platforms
and we're gonna use them.

00:28:43.440 --> 00:28:46.320
Well by now my bias
toward Atlanta is clear,

00:28:46.320 --> 00:28:48.480
but there is a massive story here.

00:28:49.120 --> 00:28:51.120
It's undeniable that something new

00:28:51.120 --> 00:28:53.760
and important happened in the past year.

00:28:54.560 --> 00:28:57.040
The fact that it happened in
Atlanta shouldn't be a surprise

00:28:57.040 --> 00:28:58.960
to anyone who knows its history,

00:28:58.960 --> 00:29:02.640
its economics, its
demographics and its politics.

00:29:02.640 --> 00:29:04.240
But few could have predicted

00:29:04.240 --> 00:29:06.400
that sports would play such a key role.

00:29:07.040 --> 00:29:09.200
Former Senator Loeffler,
remember she said,

00:29:09.200 --> 00:29:11.600
there should be less politics in sports.

00:29:11.600 --> 00:29:12.880
Having looked into this,

00:29:12.880 --> 00:29:14.960
I think it's fair to say
there's gonna be more.

00:29:16.240 --> 00:29:18.800
What happened in Atlanta
says something bigger,

00:29:19.440 --> 00:29:22.400
something about sports
and business and culture

00:29:22.400 --> 00:29:24.880
and how they're inextricably intertwined

00:29:24.880 --> 00:29:26.160
in a new and different way.

00:29:26.720 --> 00:29:29.040
It's a complicated world to be sure,

00:29:29.040 --> 00:29:30.240
but it sure is fascinating.

00:29:31.520 --> 00:29:32.640
Thanks so much for watching.

00:29:32.640 --> 00:29:34.000
Would love to hear what you think

00:29:34.000 --> 00:29:37.760
about this and other episodes
about the business of sports.

00:29:37.760 --> 00:29:39.360
I'll be in the comments section.

00:29:39.360 --> 00:29:48.800
Can't wait to hear what you think.

